    "body": "A basketball game moves quickly, and a player with the ball has only moments to choose what to do. Caitlin Clark became known for making those decisions in exciting ways. She can shoot from far beyond the three-point line, but her passing is just as important. A surprising pass may reach a teammate who has found open space near the basket. Viewers see the ball move; a point guard must also picture where defenders and teammates are likely to go next.\n\n## From Iowa to national attention\n\nCaitlin grew up in Iowa and played basketball from a young age. At the University of Iowa, she became one of the most talked-about college players in the country. In 2024, she broke the NCAA Division I career scoring record across men's and women's basketball. A scoring record is based on points made during official games, not on a player's popularity. Reaching that total required many seasons of consistent work, not just a handful of memorable long shots.\n\n## What makes a pass special?\n\nAn assist is usually credited when a player's pass leads directly to a teammate's basket. Assists matter because basketball is a team sport. A defender may focus on stopping a famous scorer, leaving someone else open. A skilled passer notices that opening. Caitlin's style often involves looking across the court rather than only at the nearest teammate. That vision takes practice, communication, and trust. The person catching the pass still has to make the shot.\n\n## A professional challenge\n\nThe Indiana Fever selected Caitlin first in the 2024 WNBA draft. Moving from college to the professional league meant facing defenders who were stronger, faster, and more experienced. A rookie must adjust to new teammates and a longer schedule while learning how opponents respond. Public attention adds pressure, but highlights do not show all the practice sessions in which a player makes ordinary improvements. A successful professional career involves learning how to respond when a plan fails.\n\n## Read a basketball box score\n\nA box score summarizes a game with numbers such as points, rebounds, assists, and turnovers. A player could score fewer points than someone else but contribute many assists that help the team. Turnovers show possessions lost through mistakes or defensive pressure. Reading these categories together gives a better picture than looking only at scoring. Good reporters explain context, including the opponent, playing time, and the outcome of the game.\n\n## The bigger lesson\n\nImagine leading a group project. You could do every task yourself, or you could recognize what each teammate does well and assign meaningful roles. Caitlin's passing offers a sports example of the second approach. Confidence matters, but so does noticing others. The next time you watch basketball, follow the player without the ball for one possession. You may discover that the open shot began with teamwork long before the pass arrived.",
