    "body": "When a basketball player scores a basket, the crowd can react instantly. Defensive work is sometimes quieter: getting into position, blocking a shot, or grabbing a rebound before an opponent can try again. A'ja Wilson of the Las Vegas Aces became one of the WNBA's outstanding players partly because she contributes on both ends of the floor. She is known for scoring, but she also protects the basket and helps her teammates organize their defense. Her story reminds fans that a great performance involves more than one number.\n\n## A South Carolina beginning\n\nA'ja grew up in South Carolina and played college basketball at the University of South Carolina. There, she helped lead the Gamecocks to the 2017 national championship. College athletes learn to balance classes, travel, practice, and games. Playing in a strong team system also means understanding assignments: when to guard a particular player, how to help when a teammate is beaten, and where to stand after a missed shot.\n\n## The first pick in the draft\n\nLas Vegas selected A'ja with the first pick in the 2018 WNBA draft. She soon became an important part of the Aces. A center often plays close to the basket, but modern centers may also shoot from farther away and pass to teammates. A'ja developed a varied set of skills, including footwork around defenders and quick reactions on defense. The better an athlete becomes, the more opponents study that athlete's habits.\n\n## Measuring a complete game\n\nPoints record scoring, rebounds show recovered missed shots, and blocks track shots stopped or changed by defenders. A'ja has excelled in all three categories. In 2024, she became the first WNBA player to score more than one thousand points in a single regular season. A major total like that is impressive, but averages, team results, and defensive statistics also help explain how a player contributed. No single measure can tell the whole story of a game.\n\n## Awards and teamwork\n\nA'ja won major league honors, including multiple Most Valuable Player awards, while the Aces became championship contenders. An MVP award recognizes outstanding performance during a season, but a championship still requires contributions from the entire roster. Players who set screens, defend difficult opponents, and come off the bench can change games even when they receive less attention. Celebrating a star does not mean forgetting the team around them.\n\n## Watch the defender\n\nDuring a basketball game, choose one possession and focus on the defensive player rather than the scorer. Notice how the defender bends their knees, moves their feet, and keeps track of nearby players. After a missed shot, watch who finds space to rebound. That activity can reveal a part of the sport that highlight videos often overlook. A'ja Wilson's career shows that excellence can mean doing difficult work on offense and defense, sometimes within the same few seconds.",
